<section class="panel" aria-label="Grade 3 Unit 4 Lesson 11 Multiplication Strategies on Ungridded Rectangles"><h2>Four original warm-up representations</h2><p>Source A gridded3×6 split2+4; B ungridded6 and12 partial areas with widths2 and4; C(3×2)+(3×4); D3 rows of6 dots. All make18 and missing side length of B is3. Partner must defend why each representation might differ.</p><h2>Ungridded source rectangles</h2><div><strong>Rectangle A, 45 square units</strong><div role="img" aria-label="Ungridded 9 by 5 rectangle" style="height:80px;width:216px;border:2px solid currentColor;display:grid;place-items:center;margin:9px 0">9×5 = 45</div></div><div><strong>Rectangle B, 36 square units</strong><div role="img" aria-label="Ungridded 6 by 6 rectangle" style="height:96px;width:144px;border:2px solid currentColor;display:grid;place-items:center;margin:9px 0">6×6 = 36</div></div><div><strong>Rectangle C, 56 square YARDS</strong><div role="img" aria-label="Ungridded 8 by 7 rectangle" style="height:112px;width:192px;border:2px solid currentColor;display:grid;place-items:center;margin:9px 0">8×7 = 56</div></div><p>Teacher checks learner-selected partitions and corresponding expressions, not just the numeric product.</p><h2>Original public 2021 card expressions A–L — first-party transcription of mathematical facts</h2><table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Original card</th><th>Expression</th><th>Area</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>A</th><td>(7×2)×2</td><td>28</td></tr><tr><th>B</th><td>6×7</td><td>42</td></tr><tr><th>C</th><td>7×4</td><td>28</td></tr><tr><th>D</th><td>8×3</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><th>E</th><td>3×6+5×6</td><td>48</td></tr><tr><th>F</th><td>(4×3)×2</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><th>G</th><td>4×(2×3)</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><th>H</th><td>4×9</td><td>36</td></tr><tr><th>I</th><td>(5×6)+(2×6)</td><td>42</td></tr><tr><th>J</th><td>2×(2×9)</td><td>36</td></tr><tr><th>K</th><td>8×6</td><td>48</td></tr><tr><th>L</th><td>(5×4)+(2×4)</td><td>28</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Sort by area: 28: A,C,L; 42: B,I; 24: D,F,G; 48: E,K; 36: H,J. Original card illustration was read on the authorized device; no publisher card graphic is embedded.</p><p>Student summary:6×5=30,30÷5=6,30÷6=5;7×3=5×3+2×3=21. Publisher Expressions for a Rectangle Cool-down remains gated. <a href="https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-3/unit-4/lesson-11/lesson.html">Original 2021 teacher lesson</a></p></section>
